{
  "version": 1,
  "canonical": "MCProxy/src/mcapp/aprs_symbol_vectors.json",
  "description": "Canonical APRS symbol vectors, shared between MCProxy (src/mcapp/aprs_symbol_tests.py) and the webapp's vendored copy. Pins how a symbol TABLE ID (aprs_symbol_group) and a symbol CODE (aprs_symbol) must survive every ingress, the SQLite round trip, the SSE serialization boundary and the sprite resolver.\n\nWhy this file exists: every suite in both repos used to stop at a seam. MCProxy's end-to-end stopped at router.publish (a Python dict); the webapp's tests began from an already-decoded JS object. No test could therefore see a value that is correct on one side of the serialization boundary and wrong on the other — which is exactly where a one-versus-two backslash lives.\n\nThis file is MCProxy-canonical and vendored to webapp/src/utils/__tests__/aprs_symbol_vectors.json. The vendored copy may differ in WHITESPACE ONLY (Prettier reformats it); drift is checked parsed, never byte-for-byte. Deliberately NOT placed in src/mcapp/contract/ — that directory is a git subtree with mc-chat upstream, so an in-place edit there is reverted by the next subtree pull, and mc-chat has no APRS symbol surface to hold in parity.",
  "sha256": "Both sides pin sha256(json.dumps(parsed, sort_keys=True, separators=(',', ':'))) of THIS document as a constant in their own suite (MCProxy: _EXPECTED_CORPUS_SHA256 in src/mcapp/aprs_symbol_tests.py). The hash lives in the suites rather than in this file because a document cannot contain its own digest. Hashing the PARSE-NORMALIZED text (not the raw bytes) is what makes Prettier's reformatting of the vendored copy invisible while a content change stays visible. Changing one vector therefore requires a commit in BOTH repos — that is the barrier.",
  "encoding_rules": {
    "values": "Every APRS symbol VALUE is a {kind} record, never a JSON string literal. kind='chars' carries `codepoints` (int array) plus a redundant `len`; both sides build the string from the codepoints and MUST assert the built length equals `len` before any behavioural assertion. No backslash literal appears in any value field of this file: [92] versus [92,92] is countable, \"\\\\\" versus \"\\\\\\\\\" is not, and that ambiguity is exactly where this bug class hides. kind='absent' means the wire key is not present at all. kind='null' and kind='non_string' carry a literal `json` value.",
    "wire": "`sse.data_line` IS a readable JSON string and DOES contain backslashes — encoding a 90-character line as codepoints would be unreadable, and unreadable fixtures rot. It is guarded instead: `sse.backslash_count` and `sse.char_len` must both be asserted against the decoded line BEFORE the line is used for anything.",
    "per_route": "`canonical` is the value after the route's INGRESS normalizer (what MCProxy publishes on `udp`, what the webapp stores on `wss`). `ble` and `store` carry route-local expectations where they differ; see `routes` below."
  },
  "routes": {
    "udp": "node -> MCProxy udp_handler._process_received_message -> router.publish. The datagram is built from `input`; the published dict must match `canonical`. This is the ONE ingress that undoubles the firmware's escape.",
    "ble": "node -> MCProxy ble_protocol.parse_aprs_position (via transform_pos). The APRS text is built as !4814.72N<group>01122.16E<code> from `input`. `ble.parses` says whether the whole position parses at all — a rejected table id costs the frame its COORDINATES, not just its symbol. When it parses, the emitted fields equal `input` verbatim: the BLE path has no normalizer and must not grow one (its single backslash is already canonical and a second pass would corrupt it).",
    "store": "`canonical` -> MCProxy storage.store_message -> station_positions -> query._build_position_dict. The read-back must match `store`. Falsy column values produce an ABSENT key, not an empty one.",
    "sse": "MCProxy sse_handler.SSEManager.format_sse_event -> webapp useSSEClient JSON.parse. The payload is built from `canonical` in the literal key order type, src, aprs_symbol, aprs_symbol_group (absent kinds omit their key) with event type 'mesh:message'. MCProxy asserts it PRODUCES `sse.data_line` byte for byte; the webapp asserts it CONSUMES it. Neither side can move alone.",
    "wss": "oevsv.at -> webapp websocket.ts parseMessage -> processDataElement.",
    "cache": "webapp offlineCache.hydratePositions -> position store (bypasses processDataElement).",
    "render": "webapp resolveSymbol / PositionListPanel. `render.resolves` is the load-bearing expectation; `render.overlay` is the character painted over the glyph ('' for the two real tables). Sprite sheet names and pixel offsets are deliberately NOT pinned here: MCProxy cannot verify them, and an unverified pin in the canonical file would fail the vendored side for a reason this repo never tested."
  },
  "wire_field_names": {
    "mcproxy": {
      "group": "aprs_symbol_group",
      "code": "aprs_symbol"
    },
    "oevsv": {
      "group": "aprs_sym_grp",
      "code": "aprs_sym"
    },
    "store": {
      "group": "aprs_sym_grp",
      "code": "aprs_sym"
    }
  },
  "route_coverage_floor": {
    "udp": 8,
    "ble": 6,
    "store": 6,
    "sse": 6,
    "wss": 6,
    "cache": 4,
    "render": 10
  },
